    {
      "id": "gxjxso9",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Background of Economic Reforms"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Economic reforms were launched in India in 1991 to resolve severe Balance of Payments (BOP) and foreign exchange crises.",
        "Liberalization dismantled the 'License-Permit Raj' and reduced state control over economic activities.",
        "Privatization encouraged private sector participation and initiated disinvestment in public sector undertakings (PSUs).",
        "Globalization integrated the Indian domestic market with the global economy through trade and investment flow reforms."
      ],
      "id": "ajm5m86"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6umgt7q",
      "content": "• In 1991, India faced a severe economic crisis."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "uty3ygq",
      "content": "• Foreign exchange reserves were very low."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2j99zlo",
      "content": "• The government introduced economic reforms to stabilize the economy."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "w30nyib",
      "content": "• The reforms aimed to promote liberalization, privatization and globalization."
    },
    {
      "id": "ho1ktyq",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Liberalization"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ygeet9t",
      "content": "• Liberalization refers to the removal of government restrictions on economic activities."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5kngfnj",
      "content": "• It encourages private sector participation."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nlrs0z4",
      "content": "• It reduces government control over industries."
    },
    {
      "id": "n6x0g7u",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Privatization"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "b4gl82w",
      "content": "• Privatization means transferring ownership of public sector enterprises to private sector."
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "Economic Reforms in India",
      "id": "fem07vh"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3gko30i",
      "content": "• It improves efficiency and productivity."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "li5b5lf",
      "content": "• It reduces the financial burden on the government."
    },
    {
      "id": "pa4asd0",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Globalization"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nz3o4dn",
      "content": "• Globalization refers to the integration of the Indian economy with the world economy."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "76ia3nk",
      "content": "• It promotes international trade and investment."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "qgxnk76",
      "content": "• It allows foreign companies to invest in India."
    },
    {
      "id": "fgx2n16",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Major Features of Economic Reforms"
    },
